\f1\fs22 Gerry Hardman stood in the secure laboratory beneath Tramp Tower, watching technicians in hazmat suits make final adjustments to what resembled ordinary air filtration units. The protest above had entered its third day, growing larger with each passing hour. What had begun as a few thousand demonstrators now threatened to exceed thirty thousand, with satellite protests emerging in other districts.\
"You're certain about containment parameters?" Gerry asked the lead scientist, a nervous man with thinning hair who kept adjusting his protective goggles.\
"Yes, Commissioner. The TMinus-Virus dispersal has been calibrated for this specific urban environment. The active radius will extend approximately two hundred meters from each deployment point, covering the entirety of the Occupy area." The scientist gestured to a holographic map where red circles indicated coverage zones. "Wind conditions are favorable. Minimal drift expected."\
Gerry nodded, his face betraying nothing of his internal calculations. He'd spent the previous night reviewing and revising his recommendation to deploy. The original plan had targeted isolated Elvi communities, not a mixed crowd of Airborne, Cloudborne, and Evolution Elvi protesters.\
"And the genetic targeting?" he asked.\
"Functioning as designed. The virus will only activate in subjects with Elvi genetic markers. Others will experience mild cold-like symptoms at worst\'97useful cover for explaining the inevitable public health response."\
CEO Dunhilton's voice crackled over the laboratory speakers. "Gerry, I'm looking at real-time satellite footage. They're setting up portable shower facilities now. Shower facilities! They're planning to stay indefinitely. I want this handled today."\
"Understood, sir," Gerry replied, his tone betraying no hesitation despite the accelerated timeline. "We're prepared to deploy immediately."\
"Good. And Gerry? Make sure our media allies receive the prepared statement about the 'unfortunate bacterial outbreak' among the protesters. Health Department is standing by to cordon off the area once symptoms manifest."\
The connection terminated with an abrupt click. Gerry turned to the operations team awaiting his command.\
"Deploy at the five designated points. Synchronize for maximum coverage." He checked his watch. "Time to dispersal: thirty minutes."\
As the team mobilized, Gerry took the private elevator back to his office, where he had a clear view of the protest below. The crowd had organized into functional zones now\'97rest areas, medical stations, food distribution, and a central gathering space where speakers took turns addressing the assembled masses. Signs bobbed above the crowd: "END THE LAYER" and "SCIENCE NOT SILENCE" among the most prominent.\
A group of Evolution Elvi led a science lesson on a small stage, demonstrating principles of natural selection using volunteers from the crowd. Nearby, Airborne and Cloudborne youths shared meals from communal cooking stations, the social barriers between them seemingly dissolved.\
Gerry's secure phone vibrated with a message: "Units in position. Awaiting final authorization."\
He watched a moment longer as a young Elvine performer led the crowd in yet another rendition of their protest anthem. The voices rose up toward his window, muffled by the soundproofed glass but still faintly audible\'97a unified chorus of discontent.\
Gerry typed a single word in response: "Proceed."\
From his vantage point, the deployment was invisible. Maintenance vehicles positioned at strategic points around the protest perimeter hummed to life, their modified ventilation systems releasing an odorless, colorless mist that dispersed rapidly in the morning air. The protesters, focused on their speeches and songs, noticed nothing.\
Gerry observed with clinical detachment, noting the locations of Evolution Elvi within the crowd. According to the briefing, symptoms would begin manifesting within twenty to forty minutes\'97mild at first, then increasingly severe for those with the targeted genetic markers.\
As he waited, he reviewed the TMinus-Virus specifications once more on his secure tablet. Unlike traditional pathogens, this engineered virus operated with precision, activating only when it encountered specific genetic sequences. For the Elvi, it would progressively shut down cellular functions, presenting initially as flu-like symptoms before rapidly accelerating toward organ failure. For everyone else, it would cause minor respiratory irritation at most\'97an acceptable level of collateral effect.\
Thirty-two minutes after deployment, Gerry noticed the first signs. An Evolution Elvi drummer faltered mid-performance, setting down his sticks to wipe sweat from his brow. Nearby, an Elvi security volunteer sat heavily on a barricade, hand pressed to his forehead. Within moments, more Evolution Elvi throughout the crowd began showing signs of distress\'97coughing, swaying unsteadily, or simply looking confused.\
The Airborne and Cloudborne protesters initially responded with concern, helping their Elvi companions to medical tents. But as more Elvi fell ill almost simultaneously, a pattern became unmistakable. Panic began rippling through the crowd as people realized something targeted was happening.\
"Health Department vehicles approaching perimeter as planned," noted Gerry's assistant via text. "Media narrative: 'Mysterious illness strikes protest, officials respond with precautionary quarantine measures.'"\
Below, chaos was spreading faster than the virus itself. Some protesters fled, while others tried to help the rapidly deteriorating Elvi. Emergency medical volunteers appeared overwhelmed as more cases presented by the minute. Health Department vehicles in hazmat gear began cordoning off streets, preventing both exit and entry.\
Gerry's phone rang. CEO Dunhilton's voice was almost cheerful. "Extraordinary effectiveness, Gerry. The board is watching the live feed. Very impressed with the precision."\
"Thank you, sir," Gerry replied. "We should have the area cleared within hours. The illness appears severe enough to warrant hospitalization for affected individuals."\
"Hospitalization? No, no\'97quarantine facilities have been prepared. Can't have them taking up valuable medical resources." Dunhilton paused. "This is working out better than expected, actually. The public seeing these genetic freaks dropping like flies while normal citizens remain healthy? Couldn't ask for better optics regarding the Elvi threat."\
As the situation deteriorated below, Gerry maintained his impassive expression despite being alone in his office. The Evolution Elvi were now being gathered into segregated treatment areas by hazmat-clad responders. Their non-Elvi allies watched helplessly, held back by barricades and warnings about contagion.\
"Initial media coverage is favorable," Dunhilton continued. "Channel Forty-Eight is already running with our talking points about terrorist biological agents potentially planted by religious extremists from the Ghetto. Beautiful misdirection."\
Gerry watched as stretchers began removing unconscious Elvi from the scene. "And the second phase, sir?"\
"Proceed as planned. Once the protest is cleared, perform decontamination theater for the cameras, then release the sanitized area back to public use." Dunhilton's tone turned reflective. "You know, Gerry, history will remember this as the day we preserved Ulyssia's natural social order. Well done."\
The line went dead. Gerry returned his attention to the scene below, where Health Department personnel were now forcing all protesters into segregated screening areas\'97Elvi showing symptoms in one section, everyone else in another. The unified movement of just hours ago had been effectively divided along precisely the lines it had sought to erase.\
He made a final note in the operation log: "Deployment successful. Target population responding as projected. Social recalibration proceeding according to timeline."\
Below him, the last Evolution Elvi performers were being loaded into quarantine transport vehicles, their instruments abandoned on the makeshift stage where, just minutes earlier, they had led the crowd in songs about unity and change.\

